Key rules that apply:
- #1 Reckon before battle: five matters, seven comparisons; ordered chain ground→measure→quantity→number→weighing→victory. Count before commit. Also: foreknowledge must come from people who know (though #11 is about spies; here "workers"/probes).
- #4 Know whether you can fight; know the other side and yourself: knowing our troops can attack but not the enemy → half a victory; knowing both but not the ground → half a victory. This is exactly the structure of my question! "Knowing our troops can attack... but not whether the ground permits battle, is still only half a victory." So my question is #4. I should structure my answer around the three tests: engines/tools (self), task as posed (enemy? or self), ground (terrain). Actually #4's three: knowing our troops can attack, knowing enemy can be attacked, knowing the ground permits. For a workflow: engines/tools = our troops; task as posed = the enemy/objective; ground = files/blueprint/information (terrain). The rule says knowing two of three is still half a victory. And "知彼知己，勝乃不殆；知天知地，勝乃可全" — know other and self → not in danger; know heaven and ground → victory can be whole.
- #2 Make yourself unbeatable first: don't rely on the enemy not coming/attacking; rely on having the means. "Do not rely on their not coming; rely on having the means to meet them." This applies to unknown spots: don't rely on U4/U5/U6 resolving themselves; build the means (fallback). Also "win first then seek battle" — establish the ground/means before committing.
- #17 Learn the enemy by probing and by signs: probe (角之) to know where surplus and shortage — corresponds to U2/U3 requiring a probe, and reading signs.
- #6 In deliberating, mix harm into benefit: name the harm side for each piece.
- #8 Value victory not duration; speed.
- #13 Not every road taken, not every command accepted — but that's more about dropping steps (Lao Tzu's question). Could use for "a spot that cannot be resolved → not every road taken"? Careful not to answer Lao Tzu's question. But it's a decision rule; I can say which pieces are "road not taken" maybe as a question to EJ. Actually the brief says do not decide for EJ.
- #3 Whole over broken; strike plans first, walled cities last: attack plans (test the design at the plan level) before building (walled cities = build, lowest). This supports: resolve at lowest cost first — probe rather than build.
- #14 Choose action by ratio of forces: with ten to one surround... "fewer, hold; outmatched, avoid" and "the stubbornness of a small force is the capture of a large one." This maps to: where our means are thin (Codex quota, one call site checked), avoid committing the whole; don't stubbornly hold a position with a small force.
- #15 general's faults: five dangers — e.g. "爱民，可烦也" (loving the people, can be harried) — hmm. The one that fits: "必生，可虜也" (set on living can be captured)? Or the general who cannot size up the enemy → rout. Could use #15 for the six defeats: "a general who cannot size up the enemy, meets many with few" = rout. If we build R2 from one measured call site, that's meeting many with few. That's a strong image: R2 built on one call site is "striking ten with one" → flight (#15, 走). Actually 走 is "with 勢 equal, striking ten with one" = using a small force against a large one. Good.
- #16 Orders clear first, then enforced; care that cannot command is useless. Not directly.
- #5 Move only on advantage, never from anger: don't move without advantage. Could apply to "don't run pieces with no gain."
- #11 Foreknowledge must come from people, pay for it: the answer keys and probes need humans/spies; "foreknowledge cannot be taken from measurement by 度" (李筌: 度, 數也) — analogy: cannot be had from arithmetic alone, must be from people who know. This is relevant to U7 (answer keys made by one explorer, blind spot) and U2 (one call site). Also "treat the converted spy generously" — pay for the ground.
- #9 Bring the other side to you; do not be brought: attack what they must rescue; seize what they cherish. Could map to: make the unknown spot come to us by setting the conditions (fixed questions/keys), rather than chasing unknowns. Hmm.
- #10 Avoid solid, strike empty; have no constant form: make the enemy show form while you show none. Adapt to the enemy — if one approach misses (getImplementationAtPosition), adapt. Also "have no constant form" — the design must adapt based on probes.
- #12 Seek it in 勢, not from the people: choose the people and rely on 勢; courage/cowardice is a matter of 勢. This maps to: if the engine/tool lacks something, adjust the ground/conditions, not blame the engine. Also relevant to U7: the explorer's blind spot is a matter of the method (勢), not the person.
- #19 Name the ground, act as prescribed: identify the ground by conditions then act. Exactly the "ground permits it" part. 死地則戰 etc. The ground here: files, blueprint, information. For each piece, name which ground it is (scattered/light/contested/etc.) and act as prescribed. And "troops with no way out fight" (投之無所往...). Hmm, could be used as: a piece in dead ground (no blueprint accepted) — dead ground: fight. But also "throw them where there's nowhere to go and they'll die rather than flee."
- #7 War is the way of deception: "when able, appear unable" — not relevant here. But "attack where unprepared" — could map to testing the unknowns where measurement is cheapest. Hmm, maybe use #7 for: appear unable — do not advertise capability. Not needed.
- #18 Spirit/heart/strength/change: "avoid keen spirit, strike when it flags"; "await disorder with order"; "do not attack crack troops". Could map to: U8 Codex quota — strike when the quota is fresh, not when it flags (don't schedule the core build on an exhausted engine). Actually #18 managing strength: "await the far-come while near, the weary while rested, the hungry while fed" — i.e., don't run the heaviest piece on the most depleted engine (Codex quota). That's a nice mapping for P2/P3 which depend on Codex.
